  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Given name then family name. Most Poles carry Catholic or old Slavic first names (Jan, Anna, Wojciech, Bogumiła) and many still mark their name day (imieniny). Surnames ending in -ski, -cki and -dzki change for women: Kowalski becomes Kowalska, Zieliński becomes Zielińska; the lists give the male form. Names like Nowak, Wójcik, Mazur and Krawczyk stay the same for women. Friends use short forms all the time: Kasia for Katarzyna, Gosia for Małgorzata, Tomek for Tomasz, Kuba for Jakub, Ola for Aleksandra. Ukrainians and Belarusians write their names in their passport's Latin spelling; Ukrainian names ending in -enko, -uk or -chuk are the same for women, while -skyi becomes -ska. Vietnamese Poles put the family name first at home (Nguyễn Văn Minh) and many also use a Polish first name at work.",
